import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How to Compress Images for the Web - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "Learn how to shrink image file sizes without losing visible quality, and which format to pick for photos, screenshots and logos.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-compress-images-for-the-web.html">
<aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Image compression</p>
      <h1>How to Compress Images for the Web</h1>
      <p class="lead">Large images slow down web pages, use up mobile data and hurt search rankings. Here is how to make them smaller without making them look worse.</p>
      <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>

      <h2>Why image size matters</h2>
      <p>Images are often the heaviest part of a web page. Smaller files load faster, which keeps visitors from leaving and helps your page perform better in search. A photo straight from a phone camera can be 3 to 10 MB. With the right settings it can fall below 300 KB and look almost identical on screen.</p>

      <h2>Choose the right format</h2>
      <p>Each format suits a different kind of image:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>JPEG</strong> works well for photos with smooth color gradients. It does not support transparency.</li>
        <li><strong>WebP</strong> usually gives smaller files than JPEG or PNG at similar quality, and supports transparency. Modern browsers all support it.</li>
        <li><strong>PNG</strong> is lossless, so it suits screenshots, text and images with sharp edges. It produces larger files for photos.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Pick a quality setting</h2>
      <p>Quality settings from 70 to 85 are a good starting point for most photos. Lower values save more space but can add visible blur or blocky patches. Compress once, then compare the result at full size. If you can see artifacts, raise the quality a little.</p>

      <h2>Resize before you compress</h2>
      <p>Many images are far bigger than they need to be. If an image displays at 800 pixels wide on your page, there is no benefit to uploading a 4000-pixel version. Resizing first is often the biggest saving you can make.</p>

      <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
      <p>Our <a href="../tools/image-compressor.html">Image Compressor</a> lets you convert, resize and compress JPEG, PNG and WebP files. It runs entirely in your browser, so your photos are never uploaded.</p>

      <!-- AdSense: place your ad unit code here once your site is approved. -->
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>A large phone photo of several megabytes can often shrink to a few hundred kilobytes at a quality of about 75 to 85 and a width matched to where it appears on the page. Always check the result at the size it will be shown.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Which quality setting should I use?</summary><p>For photos, start between 75 and 85. Lower values save space but can show blocky patches, so compare at full size before publishing.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Should I resize before or after compressing?</summary><p>Resize first. A photo shown 800 pixels wide does not need 4,000 pixels, and resizing removes the most data.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Which format is best for screenshots?</summary><p>PNG keeps sharp text and edges perfectly. JPG can blur text, so use it for photographs only.</p></details>
      </section>
